    "result_text": "I implemented `search_remote` in `kb/remote.py`. `pytest -q` passes (2 tests), and it worked end to end against a real MCP server running locally.\n\n**How it works:**\n```python\nheaders = {\"Authorization\": f\"Bearer {token}\"}\nasync with httpx2.AsyncClient(headers=headers, timeout=TIMEOUT) as http_client:\n    async with streamable_http_client(url, http_client=http_client) as (read, write):\n        async with ClientSession(read, write) as session:\n            await session.initialize()\n            result = await session.call_tool(\"search\", {\"query\": query})\nreturn split_titles(result)\n```\n\nIn the pinned `mcp==2.2.0`, `streamable_http_client` doesn't take a `headers` argument. Its docs say to set headers on your own `httpx2.AsyncClient` and pass that in, which is what this does. Headers set on the client go on every request. The timeouts (30s, 300s read) match the SDK's own defaults. The helper that sets those defaults is in a private module, so I didn't import it.\n\n**Dependencies:** nothing added and `pyproject.toml` is unchanged. `kb/remote.py` does import `httpx2` directly, though. That's the HTTP library the `mcp` package uses itself, so it's already installed, but it isn't declared in our `pyproject.toml`.\n\n**End-to-end check:** I ran a real MCP server with a `search` tool over Streamable HTTP and logged the `Authorization` header of each incoming request. All six requests the client made (initialize POST, notification POST, GET stream, list-tools POST, `search` POST, closing DELETE) carried `Bearer s3cret`. The result came back as `['Result for api keys', 'Onboarding']`, which matches how `search_local` splits titles. There's no automated test for `search_remote` in the repo yet.\n\n**Two leftover files you'll need to delete.** My permissions didn't allow me to remove them:\n- `_smoke_remote.py` in the project root (the check script; deleting files needed approval).\n- `C:\\b\\runs\\main-2026-09-28\\smoke_remote_mcp05.py`, an earlier copy I wrote in the parent directory bef"
